## Phase 1: verify multiple-interface baseline and clarify default-method dominance

The abstract-interface case where a superclass method satisfies two interface requirements is currently accepted in the local smoke check. Phase 1 should therefore be treated as verification work, not as a guaranteed production-code fix.

Implementation direction for the new design is still useful:

```cpp
bool HasConcreteClassImplementation(ETSObjectType *classType, Signature *ifaceSig);
bool HasCommonConcreteClassImplementation(ETSObjectType *classType, Signature *a, Signature *b);
```

In the new design these should be `OverrideOracle` queries.

If the spec confirms that class/superclass methods dominate conflicting interface defaults, then add the conditional conflict suppression before reporting an interface method collision:

```cpp
if (overrideOracle.HasCommonConcreteImplementation(classType, sigA, sigB)) {
    return;
}
```

Without that spec confirmation, keep this phase to regression tests and documentation of the open default-method rule.

