## 2. Default-interface conflict can ignore a concrete superclass implementation

The abstract-interface baseline is accepted by a local smoke check:

```ts
class C1 {
    foo(): string { return "" }
}

interface I1 {
    foo(): string
}

interface I2 {
    foo(): string
}

class C2 extends C1 implements I1, I2 {}
```

Expected semantics:

- `C2` inherits concrete `C1.foo`;
- `C1.foo` is override-compatible with `I1.foo`;
- `C1.foo` is override-compatible with `I2.foo`;
- both interface requirements are satisfied;
- no interface conflict exists.

The related default-method variant is currently rejected locally:

```ts
class C1 { foo(): string { return "" } }
interface I1 { foo(): string { return "1" } }
interface I2 { foo(): string { return "2" } }
class C2 extends C1 implements I1, I2 {}
```

Treat this rejection as a compiler deficiency under the current spec reading:

- the default-interface-method spec says a default body provides an implementation for a class **if such a class does not override the method that implements the interface**;
- no separate compile-time error was found for two interface defaults when a class/superclass method already overrides/implements the method;
- therefore `C1.foo` should dominate both `I1.foo` and `I2.foo` for `C2`, and no default conflict should remain.

The abstract-interface case remains useful as a regression test. The default-method case is the active Finding #2 production issue.

### Desired local fix

Before reporting a conflict between two inherited interface default methods, ask the override oracle whether the class hierarchy has a concrete method that implements both:

```cpp
if (overrideOracle.HasCommonConcreteImplementation(classType, sigA, sigB)) {
    return; // no interface conflict; class hierarchy resolves both defaults
}
```

---

