# Is proposed design enough?

## 1. Missing CTE: multiple methods override same superclass method

Example:

```ts
class A {}
class B extends A {}

class Base {
    foo(p: B) {}
}

class Derived extends Base {
    foo(p: B) {}
    foo(p: A) {}
}
```

Spec basis:

> If more than one method of the subclass overrides the same method of the superclass, a compile-time error occurs.

The proposed `OverrideOracle::GetOverriddenTargets` plus duplicate validation can fix this.

But the design must be precise:

> Duplicate override validation must inspect the **effective method set of the subclass**, not only own methods.

This matters for tests like:

```ts
class Base {
    foo(p: B) {}
}

class Mid extends Base {
    foo(p: A) {}
}

class Derived extends Mid {
    foo(p: B) {}
}
```

Here `Derived` has:

- inherited `Mid.foo(A)`;
- declared `Derived.foo(B)`;

and both can override `Base.foo(B)`.

So this is fixable by the proposed design, but the algorithm should be stated as:

```cpp
for (auto *methodSig : EffectiveInstanceMethodSignatures(type)) {
    for (auto target : overrideOracle.GetOverriddenTargets(type, methodSig)) {
        overriddenBy[target.baseViewSignature].push_back(methodSig);
    }
}

if (overriddenBy[target].size() > 1) {
    report CTE;
}
```

not only:

```cpp
OwnMethodSignatures(type)
```

## Verdict

✅ Covered, with one tightening: use **effective own + inherited methods**, with generic substitution.

---

## 2. Concrete superclass method dominates interface defaults

Example shape:

```ts
interface I1 {
    foo(): string { return "I1.foo" }
}

interface I2 {
    foo(): string { return "I2.foo" }
}

class C1 implements I1, I2 {
    foo(): string { return "C1.foo" }
}

class C2 extends C1 implements I3, I4 {}
```

Several suppressed tests fail because the checker reports interface conflicts even though the class hierarchy already has a concrete method.

This is exactly what our Finding #2 covers:

```cpp
overrideOracle.HasCommonConcreteImplementation(classType, sigA, sigB)
```

Before reporting an interface-default conflict, the checker should ask whether the class/superclass chain provides one concrete implementation that overrides both interface defaults.

## Verdict

✅ Covered by proposed `OverrideOracle`.

---

## 3. Interface default methods overriding other interface defaults

Example from `methods_overriding.ets`:

```ts
interface A {
    m(): int { return 5 }
}

interface B extends A {
    m(): int { return 10 }
}

class C implements B {
    public bar(): int {
        return this.m()
    }
}
```

Expected:

```ts
new C().bar() == 10
```

Current verifier/linker sees both defaults:

```text
Conflicting default implementations B.m A.m
```

This is **not fixed just by `HasCommonConcreteImplementation`**, because there is no concrete class method.

The relation is:

```text
B.m overrides A.m in interface B
```

The frontend/runtime metadata must ensure only the most specific applicable default is considered.

The proposed `OverrideOracle` can answer this relation, but the design currently needs an explicit consumer:

```cpp
DefaultImplementationResolver
```

or `OverrideOracle` methods such as:

```cpp
ArenaVector<DefaultImplementation> GetApplicableInterfaceDefaults(
    ETSObjectType *classType,
    MethodKey key);

DefaultResolution ResolveDefaultImplementation(
    ETSObjectType *classType,
    MethodKey key);
```

It must:

1. collect applicable interface defaults;
2. remove defaults overridden by more specific interface defaults;
3. account for superclass-selected defaults;
4. report conflict only if more than one unresolved default remains;
5. drive lowering/emission so verifier does not see conflicting defaults.

## Verdict

⚠️ Not fully covered as currently written.

✅ Covered if we add **default interface implementation resolution** as an explicit oracle consumer.

---

## 4. Superclass-selected interface default dominates later interface default

Example from `deep_inheritance_overriding_positive_9.ets`:

```ts
interface I1 {
    foo(): A1 { return new A1() }
}

class C1 implements I1, I2 {}

interface I3 {
    foo(): A1 { return new A2() }
}

class C2 extends C1 implements I3 {}
```

The test expects `C2.foo()` to use the implementation selected for `C1`, i.e. `I1.foo`.

This is another case not covered by **concrete class method only** logic.

The implementation must model an inherited implementation as one of:

```text
concrete class method
or selected default interface method from superclass
```

So the query should be broader than:

```cpp
HasCommonConcreteImplementation(...)
```

We need something like:

```cpp
ImplementationTarget ResolveImplementation(ETSObjectType *classType, Signature *requirement);
bool HasCommonImplementation(ETSObjectType *classType, Signature *a, Signature *b);
```

where `ImplementationTarget` may be:

```cpp
enum class ImplementationKind {
    CLASS_METHOD,
    SUPERCLASS_METHOD,
    INTERFACE_DEFAULT,
};
```

## Verdict

⚠️ Not fully covered by current “concrete implementation” wording.

✅ Covered if `OverrideOracle` / default resolver models **selected inherited default implementations**, not only concrete methods.

---

## 5. Inherited superinterface methods with override-compatible signatures

Examples:

```ts
class Base {}
class Derived extends Base {}

interface I1 {
    foo(p: Base) {}
}

interface I2 {
    foo(p: Derived) {}
}

interface I3 extends I1, I2 {}
```

and:

```ts
interface I1 {
    foo(): Base { return new Base() }
}

interface I2 {
    foo(): Derived { return new Derived() }
}

interface I3 extends I1, I2 {}
```

Current compiler reports `ESE0187`.

Expected: no conflict if one inherited interface method override-dominates the other.

This requires `OverrideOracle` to compare **inherited interface candidates**, not only methods declared directly in the current interface.

So `CheckInterfaceFunctions` should stop doing flat pairwise collision checks over all collected interface methods and instead ask:

```cpp
overrideOracle.DoesInterfaceCandidateDominate(candidateA, candidateB, viewType)
```

or build the effective inherited interface method set through the oracle.

## Verdict

✅ Covered by proposed design conceptually.

⚠️ Needs explicit statement: interface validation must use **effective inherited interface method views**, including inherited candidates that override other inherited candidates.

---

## 6. Generics

Examples:

```ts
interface I1<U1 extends A2> {
    foo(p: U1): A1 | A2 { return new A1() }
}

interface I2<U2 extends A1> {
    foo(p: U2): A1 | A2 { return new A2() }
}

class C1<V1 extends A2> implements I1<V1>, I2<V1> {
    foo(p: V1): A1 | A2 { return new A2() }
}
```

and negative cases such as:

```ts
class Root<T extends B> {
    foo(p: T): void {}
}

class Mid<T extends B> extends Root<T> {
    foo(p: A): void {}
}

class Leaf<T extends B> extends Mid<T> {
    foo(p: T): void {}
}
```

These need substituted inherited signatures.

Our `GenericViewResolver` addition is exactly required here.

But it must be used by:

- override compatibility;
- duplicate override validation;
- interface default conflict resolution;
- default implementation resolution;
- bridge/default-method lowering.

## Verdict

✅ Covered by proposed generic view design, if applied consistently to default resolution too.

