## Updated proposed design: stateless lazy semantic relation services

## Why stateless/lazy instead of stored override data?

Permanently stored override data on AST nodes or object types has an invalidation problem:

- plugins can modify AST;
- `Recheck` can rebuild signatures;
- generic substitutions depend on viewpoint;
- type relation caches can become stale;
- overload sets and call targets depend on the current semantic world.

A stateless/lazy oracle model is safer:

- AST/type nodes do not own long-lived override relation data;
- the checker owns short-lived relation caches;
- caches are tied to checker-run lifetime and explicit clearing;
- caches are cleared after plugin mutation barriers and during `Recheck`;
- callers ask semantic questions when needed.

This is inspired by the existing `TypeRelation` style: relation answers are cached, but the relation is not stored permanently on every node.

## SemanticRelationCache

Introduce a checker-owned manager:

```cpp
class SemanticRelationCache {
public:
    explicit SemanticRelationCache(ETSChecker *checker);

    void Clear();

    OverrideOracle &Overrides();
    OverloadSetOracle &Overloads();

private:
    ETSChecker *checker_ {};
    OverrideOracle overrideOracle_;
    OverloadSetOracle overloadOracle_;
};
```

In the short-term design, cache correctness comes from strict clearing at `Recheck` and plugin mutation barriers. An epoch/generation counter is optional: useful as a debug guard or later for fine-grained LSP/plugin incremental invalidation, but not required if caches are never reused across those barriers.

## OverrideOracle

The override oracle answers override and implementation questions lazily:

```cpp
class OverrideOracle {
public:
    OverrideDecision CheckOverride(Signature *base, Signature *derived, OverrideContext ctx);

    ArenaVector<OverrideTarget> GetOverriddenTargets(ETSObjectType *owner, Signature *derived);

    bool HasConcreteImplementation(ETSObjectType *classType, Signature *interfaceSig);

    bool HasCommonConcreteImplementation(ETSObjectType *classType, Signature *a, Signature *b);

    void Clear();
};
```

Important query types:

- pair compatibility cache;
- inherited method candidate cache;
- overridden targets cache;
- common concrete implementation cache.

## OverloadSetOracle

The overload-set oracle builds overload sets on demand and caches them until the next `Recheck` or plugin cache clear.

```cpp
enum class OverloadSetKind {
    FUNCTION,
    INSTANCE_METHOD,
    STATIC_METHOD,
    INTERFACE_METHOD,
    CONSTRUCTOR,
    EXPLICIT_ALIAS,
};

struct OverloadSetKey {
    ETSObjectType *receiverType;   // null for free functions
    varbinder::Scope *scope;       // for free functions / namespace functions
    util::StringView name;
    OverloadSetKind kind;
};

struct OverloadCandidate {
    Signature *signature;
    varbinder::LocalVariable *variable;
    ir::AstNode *declaration;
    CandidateOrigin origin;        // own, explicit, superclass, interface
    size_t order;
};

class OverloadSetOracle {
public:
    const OverloadSet &GetOverloadSet(const OverloadSetKey &key);
    const OverloadSet &ExpandExplicitOverload(ir::OverloadDeclaration *decl);
    void Clear();
};
```

Responsibilities:

- build free-function overload sets from scope declarations;
- build instance-method overload sets in spec order: own candidates, direct superclass set, direct interface sets in `implements` order;
- build interface-method overload sets in spec order: own candidates, direct superinterface sets in `extends` order;
- build static-method overload sets from directly declared static methods only;
- build constructor overload sets, including anonymous constructors and explicit named constructor overloads;
- expand listed names in explicit overload declarations, including names that denote implicit overload groups;
- use `OverrideOracle` queries to skip inherited candidates already represented by overriding methods;
- preserve candidate declaration/listing order for first-match overload resolution.

Overload sets should generally be lazy because most possible member-name/type combinations are never used. A call expression asks for one overload set, validates candidates against its arguments, and stores the selected target. The cache is discarded on `Recheck` or plugin mutation barriers.

## Generic substitution context matters

A cache key must not be only:

```cpp
{ baseSignaturePointer, derivedSignaturePointer }
```

because inherited generic signatures may need to be viewed through a derived instantiation.

Example:

```ts
class B<T extends Object> {
    foo(p: T) {}
}

class D extends B<string> {
    foo(p: string) {}
}
```

The oracle should model inherited candidates as:

```cpp
struct InheritedMethodCandidate {
    Signature *originalSignature; // declaration signature
    Signature *viewSignature;     // substituted as seen from derived type
    ETSObjectType *declaringType;
    ETSObjectType *viewOwner;
};
```

Then relation checks use the correct view signature and context.

