## When default implementation resolution is needed

Default implementation resolution is needed for:

- class/interface validation before reporting `ESE0187` interface method collisions;
- selecting the effective default method inherited from a subinterface that overrides a superinterface default;
- deciding whether a superclass-selected default continues to satisfy a subclass requirement;
- avoiding verifier/class-linker failures from multiple emitted default implementations;
- runtime dispatch through interface references when no concrete class method exists;
- generic interface defaults after substitution.

The resolver should be invoked before lowering/emission commits to a default-method representation. A class should expose either a concrete implementation, one selected interface default, or a checker diagnostic; it should not rely on the verifier to discover unresolved default conflicts.

