## Phase 1: local default-interface conflict fix

Keep the abstract-interface case where a superclass method satisfies two interface requirements as a regression test.

The active fix is the default-interface case: if two inherited interface defaults have the same method and the class/superclass chain already provides a concrete implementation, the checker should not report an interface conflict.

Implementation direction:

```cpp
bool HasConcreteClassImplementation(ETSObjectType *classType, Signature *ifaceSig);
bool HasCommonConcreteClassImplementation(ETSObjectType *classType, Signature *a, Signature *b);
```

In the new design these should be `OverrideOracle` queries.

Before reporting an interface default method collision:

```cpp
if (overrideOracle.HasCommonConcreteImplementation(classType, sigA, sigB)) {
    return;
}
```

This can be a small, low-risk patch while the larger oracle migration is prepared.

