In the short-term design, cache correctness comes from strict clearing at `Recheck` and plugin mutation barriers. An epoch/generation counter is optional: useful as a debug guard or later for fine-grained LSP/plugin incremental invalidation, but not required if caches are never reused across those barriers.

## OverrideOracle

The override oracle answers override and implementation questions lazily:

```cpp
class OverrideOracle {
public:
    OverrideDecision CheckOverride(Signature *baseViewSignature, Signature *derivedSignature);

    ArenaVector<OverrideTarget> GetOverriddenTargets(ETSObjectType *owner, Signature *derived);

    bool HasConcreteImplementation(ETSObjectType *classType, Signature *interfaceSig);

    bool HasCommonConcreteImplementation(ETSObjectType *classType, Signature *a, Signature *b);

    void Clear();
};
```

Important query types:

- pair compatibility cache;
- inherited method candidate cache;
- overridden targets cache;
- common concrete implementation cache.

## DefaultImplementationResolver

The suppressed overriding tests show that override compatibility alone is not enough. Interface default methods also need a single semantic resolution path. Otherwise es2panda can emit bytecode that later fails verifier/class-linker checks with conflicting default implementations.

Default implementation resolution should be checker-owned and lazy, either as a separate service under `SemanticRelationCache` or as a clearly separated `OverrideOracle` subcomponent:

```cpp
enum class ImplementationKind {
    CLASS_METHOD,
    SUPERCLASS_METHOD,
    INTERFACE_DEFAULT,
};

struct ImplementationTarget {
    ImplementationKind kind;
    Signature *signature;
    ir::AstNode *declaration;
    ETSObjectType *declaringType;
};

class DefaultImplementationResolver {
public:
    ArenaVector<ImplementationTarget> GetApplicableDefaults(ETSObjectType *classType, MethodKey key);

    ImplementationResolution ResolveImplementation(ETSObjectType *classType, Signature *requirement);

    bool HasCommonImplementation(ETSObjectType *classType, Signature *a, Signature *b);

    void Clear();
};
```

Responsibilities:

- prefer concrete class/superclass methods over interface defaults when they override/implement the requirement;
- collect applicable interface default methods when no class implementation exists;
- remove default methods overridden by more specific subinterface defaults;
- detect real unresolved multiple-default conflicts only after dominance filtering;
- model selected inherited defaults from superclasses, not only concrete class methods;
- provide lowering/emission with one selected default implementation or an explicit conflict diagnostic;
- use generic view signatures when the default comes from a parameterized interface.

This service consumes `OverrideOracle` rather than duplicating override logic. It is the missing semantic bridge between checker diagnostics and verifier-safe default-method emission.

## OverloadSetOracle

The overload-set oracle builds overload sets on demand and caches them until the next `Recheck` or plugin cache clear.

```cpp
enum class OverloadSetKind {
    FUNCTION,
    INSTANCE_METHOD,
    STATIC_METHOD,
    INTERFACE_METHOD,
    CONSTRUCTOR,
    EXPLICIT_ALIAS,
};

struct OverloadSetKey {
    ETSObjectType *receiverType;   // null for free functions
    varbinder::Scope *scope;       // for free functions / namespace functions
    util::StringView name;
    OverloadSetKind kind;
};

struct OverloadCandidate {
    Signature *signature;
    varbinder::LocalVariable *variable;
    ir::AstNode *declaration;
    CandidateOrigin origin;        // own, explicit, superclass, interface
    size_t order;
};

class OverloadSetOracle {
public:
    const OverloadSet &GetOverloadSet(const OverloadSetKey &key);
    const OverloadSet &ExpandExplicitOverload(ir::OverloadDeclaration *decl);
    void Clear();
};
```

Responsibilities:

- build free-function overload sets from scope declarations;
- build instance-method overload sets in spec order: own candidates, direct superclass set, direct interface sets in `implements` order;
- build interface-method overload sets in spec order: own candidates, direct superinterface sets in `extends` order;
- build static-method overload sets from directly declared static methods only;
- build constructor overload sets, including anonymous constructors and explicit named constructor overloads;
- expand listed names in explicit overload declarations, including names that denote implicit overload groups;
- use `OverrideOracle` queries to skip inherited candidates already represented by overriding methods;
- preserve candidate declaration/listing order for first-match overload resolution.

Overload sets should generally be lazy because most possible member-name/type combinations are never used. A call expression asks for one overload set, validates candidates against its arguments, and stores the selected target. The cache is discarded on `Recheck` or plugin mutation barriers.

## Generic substitution context matters

A cache key must not be only:

```cpp
{ baseSignaturePointer, derivedSignaturePointer }
```

because inherited generic signatures may need to be viewed through a derived instantiation.

Example:

```ts
class B<T extends Object> {
    foo(p: T) {}
}

class D extends B<string> {
    foo(p: string) {}
}
```

The oracle should model inherited candidates as:

```cpp
struct InheritedMethodCandidate {
    Signature *originalSignature; // declaration signature
    Signature *viewSignature;     // substituted as seen from derived type
    ETSObjectType *declaringType;
    ETSObjectType *viewOwner;
};
```

Then relation checks use the correct view signature and context.

## Generics-specific obligations

Generics must be a first-class part of the oracle design. Override and overload answers for generic supertypes are often **viewpoint-dependent**: the same declaration signature can look different when inherited through a particular subclass or interface instantiation.

Example:

```ts
class B<T extends Object> {
    foo(p: T): T { return p }
}

class D extends B<string> {
    override foo(p: string): string { return p }
}
```

The implementation must distinguish:

```text
original declaration signature:  B.foo(T): T
signature as seen from D:        B.foo(string): string
erased/runtime descriptor:       descriptor based on effective/erased types
```

Design consequences:

- `OverrideOracle` must compare a derived method against the inherited method **view signature**, not blindly against the original generic declaration signature.
- `OverloadSetOracle` must add inherited generic methods as candidates using the correct substituted view from the receiver type.
- `DefaultImplementationResolver` must resolve parameterized interface defaults using the correct substituted default-method signature view.
- `NeedsBridge(baseViewSignature, derivedSignature)` must consider generic erasure and substituted inherited signatures.
- Explicit overload expansion must preserve generic candidates rather than eagerly instantiate or drop them.
- Call resolution over generic overload candidates must still perform explicit type-argument filtering, optional/default type-parameter handling, and implicit type inference per candidate.

A useful helper layer is:

```cpp
class GenericViewResolver {
public:
    Signature *ViewSignatureFrom(ETSObjectType *derivedType,
                                 ETSObjectType *declaringType,
                                 Signature *signature);

    ETSObjectType *ViewTypeFrom(ETSObjectType *derivedType,
                                ETSObjectType *declaringType);
};
```

The checker should also cache substituted signature views to avoid repeated allocation and substitution:

```cpp
struct SignatureViewKey {
    Signature *original;
    ETSObjectType *viewOwner;
    // or a canonical substitution identity if needed later
};
```

Generic type-parameter constraints require special care. The spec wording around constraint variance should be clarified with spec owners if possible; until then, implementation should consistently follow the current spec examples and existing `TypeRelation` behavior.

Minimum generic-specific test families:

- generic class method override, e.g. `D extends B<string>` overriding `B<T>.foo(T)`;

