```ts
class C1 { foo(): string { return "" } }
interface I1 { foo(): string { return "1" } }
interface I2 { foo(): string { return "2" } }
class C2 extends C1 implements I1, I2 {}
```

Expected semantics:

- `C2` inherits concrete `C1.foo`;
- `C1.foo` is override-compatible with `I1.foo`;
- `C1.foo` is override-compatible with `I2.foo`;
- the two interface defaults are not needed as implementations;
- no interface-default conflict remains.

Spec basis: an interface default body provides an implementation for a class only if that class does not override the method that implements the interface. Here the class hierarchy already provides the implementation.

The abstract-interface variant should remain as regression coverage, but it is not the active Finding #2 issue.

### Required semantic query

Before reporting a conflict between two inherited interface default methods, validation must ask whether one implementation already resolves both requirements. In the full design, this is one case of `MethodImplementationResolver::HasCommonImplementation`:

```cpp
if (implementationResolver.HasCommonImplementation(classType, sigA, sigB)) {
    return; // no interface conflict; one implementation resolves both defaults
}

