Problems:

- checker and lowering may disagree;
- the two bridge lowerings may disagree with each other;
- bridge conditions can be too narrow;
- deduplication is harder;
- diagnostics and emitted methods are based on different analyses.

### Desired change

Bridge generation should have one oracle-driven planning path. It may still reuse existing helper code for bridge body construction, but it should not keep two independent semantic discovery algorithms.

Bridge planning should consume the same override oracle as checker diagnostics:

```cpp
for (auto *methodSig : OwnConcreteMethodSignatures(classType)) {
    for (auto target : overrideOracle.GetOverriddenTargets(classType, methodSig)) {
        if (NeedsBridge(target.base, methodSig)) {
            GenerateBridge(target.base, methodSig);
        }
    }
}
```

This is relation traversal through the oracle; no long-lived override data needs to be stored on the type. The desired end state is either a single physical bridge lowering phase or a thin coordinator that owns all bridge planning and delegates only mechanical body construction to generic/override-specific helpers.

