The design is now much better, but several things are still **missed / under-specified**.

## 1. `MethodKey` is not defined

We now rely on:

```cpp
ResolveImplementation(ETSObjectType *classType, MethodKey key)
```

but the design does not define what `MethodKey` is.

This is important because `MethodKey` decides which methods/defaults belong to one implementation group.

It probably must include:

```cpp
struct MethodKey {
    util::StringView name;
    EffectiveSignature parameterSignature;
    MethodKind kind;        // method / getter / setter?
    bool isStatic;
};
```

Open question: should return type be part of `MethodKey`?

For override/default conflict grouping, probably **no**, because return type participates in override compatibility but does not identify overload-equivalence. But this must be stated.

## 2. Accessors and optional interface properties are not properly covered

The design mostly talks about methods.

But existing checker code handles:

- getters;
- setters;
- optional properties;
- interface properties with default-like behavior;
- method/property collision diagnostics.

Spec says overriding rules also apply to accessors in some contexts.

So the design should explicitly say whether services support:

```cpp
MethodKind::METHOD
MethodKind::GETTER
MethodKind::SETTER
```

or whether properties/accessors are out of scope.

Currently this is missed.

## 3. Private interface default methods are not handled

The spec allows private default methods in interfaces.

These should not participate in normal public interface implementation/default resolution in the same way as public defaults.

The design mentions accessible methods generally, but does not spell out:

- private interface default is not inherited as public implementation;
- private default should not satisfy class/interface requirements;
- private default should not create public default conflicts;
- private default may still be callable from other interface default bodies.

This should be explicit.

## 4. Static methods are not fully separated

Static methods do not participate in instance override dispatch.

The design mentions this in spec summary, but the service APIs do not force it.

`MethodView` or `MethodKey` should carry:

```cpp
bool isStatic;
```

or a stronger enum:

```cpp
enum class MemberDispatchKind {
    INSTANCE,
    STATIC,
};
```

Otherwise implementation can accidentally run override/default logic over static methods.

## 5. `this` return type handling is under-specified

Override-compatible signatures have special rules for return type `this`.

The design says `MethodView` has `viewOwner`, but does not explicitly say:

> `this` in an inherited signature must be interpreted from the correct view owner before override compatibility is checked.

This matters for examples like:

```ts
interface I {
    clone(): this
}

class C implements I {
    clone(): this { return this }
}
```

and for generic/interface inheritance.

## 6. Interface default resolution needs exact diamond rules

We added group-based method implementation resolution, but did not fully define diamond behavior.

Cases to specify:

### Same default inherited through two paths

```ts
interface A { foo(): string { return "A" } }
interface B extends A {}
interface C extends A {}
class D implements B, C {}
```

Should not be a conflict if it is literally the same default declaration.

### More specific default overrides one inherited default

```ts
interface A { foo(): string { return "A" } }
interface B extends A { foo(): string { return "B" } }
class C implements B {}
```

Should select `B.foo`.

### Two unrelated defaults remain

```ts
interface A { foo(): string { return "A" } }
interface B { foo(): string { return "B" } }
class C implements A, B {}
```

Should be a real conflict unless class/superclass supplies implementation.

The design states this generally, but does not define the exact deduplication key:

```cpp
DefaultIdentityKey {
    original declaration;
    declaring interface;
    substituted view;
}
```

That is missing.

## 7. Superclass-selected default semantics need clearer representation

We now mention “preserve superclass-selected default”, but not how to represent it.

Example:

```ts
interface I1 { foo(): string { return "I1" } }
class C1 implements I1 {}

interface I2 { foo(): string { return "I2" } }
class C2 extends C1 implements I2 {}
```

If `C1` has selected `I1.foo`, does `C2` inherit that as an implementation target?

The design should introduce an implementation kind like:

```cpp
enum class ImplementationKind {
    CLASS_METHOD,
    SUPERCLASS_METHOD,
    INTERFACE_DEFAULT,
    INHERITED_SELECTED_DEFAULT,
    NONE,
    CONFLICT,
};
```

Currently `ImplementationKind` has only:

```cpp
CLASS_METHOD,
SUPERCLASS_METHOD,
INTERFACE_DEFAULT
```

That is probably insufficient.

## 8. Default emission planning is named but not designed

We added:

```cpp
DefaultEmissionPlanner
```

but did not define what it can produce.

It should have an explicit result model:

```cpp
enum class DefaultEmissionAction {
    NONE,
    EMIT_FORWARDER,
    MARK_SELECTED_DEFAULT,
    SUPPRESS_OVERRIDDEN_DEFAULT,
    ERROR_ALREADY_REPORTED,
};
```

Without this, the boundary between semantic resolution and bytecode/verifier-safe representation is still vague.

This is important because many ignored tests fail in verifier/classlinker, not only checker.

## 9. Bridge planning and default forwarder planning may conflict

We separated bridge planning from default emission, but did not define interaction.

A selected default method may itself require:

- a forwarding method;
- an adapter for erased/generic signature;
- descriptor deduplication;
- synthetic method flags.

Question:

> Is a default forwarder also allowed to need bridge-like adaptation?

If yes, bridge planner and default emission planner need a shared helper-generation / descriptor-dedup layer.

Currently missed.

## 10. `EffectiveInstanceMethodViews(type)` is not defined

Several examples now use:

```cpp
EffectiveInstanceMethodViews(type)
```

But this is a critical primitive.

It must define whether it includes:

- own declared methods;
- inherited superclass methods;
- inherited interface defaults;
- synthetic/bridge methods;
- abstract methods;
- private methods;
- static methods;
- accessors;
- explicit overload aliases.

For duplicate override diagnostics, it should include own + inherited methods that are visible/effective in the class view, but exclude synthetic bridge/default helper methods.

This needs a precise definition.

## 11. Explicit overloads + implementation resolution interaction is missing

We designed `OverloadSetOracle` and `MethodImplementationResolver` separately.

But interfaces can have overloaded/default methods, including explicit overload declarations.

Need to define behavior for:

```ts
interface I {
    foo(x: number): string { return "" }
    foo(x: string): string { return "" }
}
```

and explicit overload aliases involving defaults.

Questions:

- Does implementation resolution run per `MethodKey` candidate?
- Does explicit overload alias inherit selected defaults?
- Can a class method implement several overloaded default methods?
- How are explicit overload declarations in interfaces handled with selected defaults?

Currently missed.

## 12. Diagnostics strategy is not designed

The design says “log conflict”, but diagnostics are a big part of correctness.

Need deterministic diagnostics for:

- duplicate override-of-same-base;
- unresolved multiple defaults;
- incompatible default vs class method;
- generic substitution conflict;
- method/property collision;
- same default inherited through multiple paths, no error;
- more specific default selected, no error.

Need to specify:

```text
which source location?
which interface names?
which method declaration path?
report one diagnostic or all pairs?
```

Otherwise implementation may pass/fail tests unpredictably.

## 13. Existing ignored test mapping is not yet incorporated

We discussed 33 suppressed overriding tests, but the docs do not yet include a compact mapping like:

| Bug family | Ignored tests | Required service |
|---|---|---|
| duplicate override CTE | `several_methods_override_negative`, `generic_complex...` | `OverrideOracle` |
| default override in interfaces | `methods_overriding`, `overriding_public_method` | `MethodImplementationResolver` |
| verifier default conflicts | `deep_inheritance...positive_2/6/...` | resolver + emission planner |
| generics | `deep_inheritance_overriding_generics_*` | `GenericViewResolver` + resolver |

This mapping would make the design more grounded.

## 14. Workload estimate may now undercount emission planning

We added `DefaultEmissionPlanner` conceptually, but the workload row still says:

```text
Method implementation resolver + default emission planning integration: 600-1000 LOC
```

That might be low if emission requires:

- synthetic forwarder generation;
- descriptor deduplication;
- class/interface metadata changes;
- bridge interaction;
- verifier-specific fixes.

Maybe keep it, but mark it high-risk or expand to:

```text
800-1400 LOC / 1.6-2.8 MM
```

depending on how much existing default emission infrastructure exists.

## 15. Spec uncertainty remains around default conflict behavior

We are treating class/superclass implementation dominance as correct, based on current spec reading.

But the spec text for default methods is minimal. It does not fully define all conflict resolution cases.

The docs should mark this as:

> implementation follows current spec reading; if spec owners clarify differently, resolver rules must be adjusted.

Not as an open blocker, but as a traceability note.

