### MethodKey rules

`MethodKey` defines the implementation/default-resolution group. It must be stable and source-semantic:

- include method/accessor name;
- include method kind: method, getter, setter;
- include instance/static dispatch kind;
- include effective parameter signature for methods and setters;
- exclude return type from grouping, because return type is checked by override compatibility and cannot distinguish overload-equivalent methods.

For accessors:

- getter key has no value parameter and is grouped by property name;
- setter key includes the value parameter effective type;
- getter/setter pairs must remain distinguishable from ordinary methods;
- optional interface properties and generated accessors must be represented as accessor views or explicitly kept out of method implementation resolution with a separate property resolver.

### MethodView filtering rules

`EffectiveInstanceMethodViews(type)` must be defined precisely. For override/default validation it should include:

- own declared instance methods/accessors;
- inherited superclass methods that remain visible/effective in the current class view;
- selected inherited default implementations represented by `MethodImplementationResolver` when they satisfy interface requirements.

It should exclude:

- static methods;
- private superinterface defaults from public implementation/default resolution;
- private superclass methods that are not accessible override candidates;
- synthetic bridge/default-forwarder/helper methods generated after checking;
- explicit overload alias declarations, because aliases are not implementation bodies.

Static methods should use separate static lookup/validation paths and must not enter instance override/default resolution.

### Private interface defaults and `this` return

Private interface default methods are implementation details of the declaring interface:

- they are not public inherited implementations;
- they do not satisfy class interface requirements;
- they do not create public default conflicts;
- they may still be callable from other default bodies according to normal accessibility rules.

Return type `this` must be interpreted through the current `MethodView::viewOwner` before checking covariance. A substituted inherited signature that returns `this` must not be compared as if `this` still belonged to the original declaring interface/class.

### Default diamond and deduplication rules

Default resolution must handle diamonds by identity and dominance:

```ts
interface A { foo(): string { return "A" } }
interface B extends A {}
interface C extends A {}
class D implements B, C {}
```

The same default declaration inherited through two paths is one implementation, not a conflict.

```ts
interface A { foo(): string { return "A" } }
interface B extends A { foo(): string { return "B" } }
class C implements B {}
```

A more specific default overrides the superinterface default; select `B.foo`.

```ts
interface A { foo(): string { return "A" } }
interface B { foo(): string { return "B" } }
class C implements A, B {}
```

Two unrelated defaults remain a conflict unless a class/superclass implementation resolves the group.

Deduplication needs a stable default identity key, for example:

```cpp
struct DefaultIdentityKey {
    ir::AstNode *originalDeclaration;
    ETSObjectType *declaringInterface;
    SupertypeViewKey view;
};
```

### Default emission plan

`DefaultEmissionPlanner` should have explicit actions rather than embedding bytecode strategy in semantic resolution:

```cpp
enum class DefaultEmissionAction {
    NONE,
    EMIT_FORWARDER,
    MARK_SELECTED_DEFAULT,
    SUPPRESS_OVERRIDDEN_DEFAULT,
    ERROR_ALREADY_REPORTED,
};

struct DefaultEmissionPlan {
    DefaultEmissionAction action;
    ImplementationTarget target;
    MethodKey key;
};
```

A selected interface default may still need an adapter/forwarder with descriptor deduplication. Bridge planning and default emission planning should share helper-body/descriptor-dedup utilities, but bridge discovery must not decide default semantics.

### Explicit overloads and defaults

Implementation resolution runs per `MethodKey`, including overloaded/default methods. Explicit overload declarations are aliases over candidate methods, not implementations themselves.

Rules:

- default methods that are part of an implicit overload group are resolved per candidate key;
- explicit overload aliases must expand to their underlying candidate methods before checking implementation/default behavior;
- a class method can implement multiple inherited overload candidates only when it is override-compatible with each target;
- selected defaults should be attached to the underlying method candidate, not to the explicit overload alias node.

### Diagnostic strategy

Diagnostics should be deterministic and group-oriented:

- duplicate override-of-same-base: report the stable base target and all derived methods that override it;
- unresolved multiple defaults: report the `MethodKey` and the minimal set of non-dominated defaults;
- same default through multiple paths: no diagnostic;
- more specific default selected: no diagnostic;
- incompatible class method vs interface requirement: report the class method and requirement after generic substitution;
- method/property or getter/setter collision: keep using dedicated diagnostics, not generic default-conflict messages.

Prefer one group diagnostic over many pairwise diagnostics unless the spec requires all pairs.

