## Generic substitution context matters

A cache key must not be only:

```cpp
{ baseSignaturePointer, derivedSignaturePointer }
```

because inherited generic signatures may need to be viewed through a derived instantiation.

Example:

```ts
class B<T extends Object> {
    foo(p: T) {}
}

class D extends B<string> {
    foo(p: string) {}
}
```

The oracle should model inherited candidates as:

```cpp
struct InheritedMethodCandidate {
    Signature *originalSignature; // declaration signature
    Signature *viewSignature;     // substituted as seen from derived type
    ETSObjectType *declaringType;
    ETSObjectType *viewOwner;
};
```

Then relation checks use the correct view signature and context.

## Generics-specific obligations

Generics must be a first-class part of the oracle design. Override and overload answers for generic supertypes are often **viewpoint-dependent**: the same declaration signature can look different when inherited through a particular subclass or interface instantiation.

Example:

```ts
class B<T extends Object> {
    foo(p: T): T { return p }
}

class D extends B<string> {
    override foo(p: string): string { return p }
}
```

The implementation must distinguish:

```text
original declaration signature:  B.foo(T): T
signature as seen from D:        B.foo(string): string
erased/runtime descriptor:       descriptor based on effective/erased types
```

Design consequences:

- override queries must compare a derived method against the inherited method **view signature**, not blindly against the original generic declaration signature.
- method overload-set queries must add inherited generic methods as candidates using the correct substituted view from the receiver type.
- implementation-resolution queries must resolve parameterized interface defaults using the correct substituted default-method signature view.
- `NeedsBridge(base.viewSignature, derived.viewSignature)` must consider generic erasure and substituted inherited signatures.
- Explicit overload expansion must preserve generic candidates rather than eagerly instantiate or drop them.
- Call resolution over generic overload candidates must still perform explicit type-argument filtering, optional/default type-parameter handling, and implicit type inference per candidate.

A useful helper layer should be based on an explicit instantiated supertype view, not only on `(derivedType, declaringType)`. The inheritance path matters for generic interfaces and repeated instantiations:

```cpp
