### Reuse existing instantiation/substitution infrastructure

Generic view support should reuse the existing checker machinery instead of creating a parallel substitution system.

Relevant existing pieces:

- `checker::InstantiationContext` validates explicit type arguments, applies defaults, checks constraints, and produces instantiated `ETSObjectType` objects;
- `ETSObjectType::Substitute()` creates instantiated object types, substitutes supertypes/interfaces, and records effective substitution data;
- instantiated `ETSObjectType` property maps can already contain substituted method signatures;
- `Signature::Substitute()` can create a substituted signature view from an existing substitution.

Recommended use:

- use `InstantiationContext` when resolving explicit generic type references or when a new instantiated object type must be created and validated;
- for override/overload/default relation queries, prefer already-instantiated `SuperType()` / `Interfaces()` path objects and existing `ETSObjectType::Substitute()` / `Signature::Substitute()` behavior;
- if a relation query needs a substitution, derive it from the instantiated supertype view or expose a small accessor/helper for the existing effective substitution, rather than maintaining another substitution map type;
- traverse real superclass/interface paths, not only transitive original-base-type sets, because repeated generic interfaces can have different instantiations along different paths.

Do not call `InstantiationContext` blindly inside relation queries just to view an inherited method: it performs validation, can log diagnostics, needs source positions, mutates/checker caches, and is oriented toward constructing type instantiations from type arguments. Relation queries usually need a diagnostic-free view of already-checked instantiated supertypes.

